# netsurf: queries on a parsed frame's document handle (patch 306)
# Generated 2026-10-19
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 305)
#
# Item 7, stage 2 (design: docs/phase-0/19). Patch 304 gave the
# parent realm a marshalled handle over a same-origin parsed
# frame's real document -- scalars and root node handles, identity
# only. Stage 2 adds the queries the design stages second:
# getElementById, querySelector, querySelectorAll and
# getElementsByTagName on the document handle.
#
#   - Each native resolves the child's CURRENT content / document /
#     generation through patch 304's qjs_fdoc_current at call time
#     and answers from the child's own libdom tree -- the same C
#     state stage 1's scalars read -- so a navigated frame's
#     queries answer the new document, and on the null document
#     (frame removed, destroyed, or not yet loaded) single lookups
#     answer null and list lookups answer empty, never a throw
#     (doc 19: "properties undefined, queries empty").
#   - Selector evaluation is the bridge's own matcher
#     (qjs_sel_list_parse / qjs_sel_list_match, querySelector's
#     single-id fast path included) run over the child's tree. The
#     matcher is a pure function of (selector, tree): no child-realm
#     JS state is consulted, no JSValue crosses a runtime, and the
#     parent never sees selector-engine state of the child's
#     (there is none to see). The natives are defined with the
#     selector machinery; only their declarations and registration
#     live with the handle.
#   - Matches are minted as patch 304 node handles capturing the
#     query call's (content, generation), so they share the roots'
#     lifetime discipline exactly: live within the generation that
#     produced them, inert after it -- a query cannot resurrect or
#     alias a stale document's nodes. Within a call, results are
#     in document order, deduped by construction (one visit per
#     node), as the realm's own querySelectorAll collects them.
#   - getElementsByTagName shares the list walk, mirroring the
#     realm's own document, whose getElementsByTagName IS
#     querySelectorAll(tag) (fixes873): one matcher, no second
#     walker. Lists answer as plain arrays of handles, as the
#     realm's own list queries answer plain arrays.
#
# Gate: testpages/pages/JS-frame-document.html extended in place,
# XDOC2 6/6 -> 8/8 (the gate script's expectation updated with
# it): check 7 runs the four queries against the first child --
# getElementById finds the element the child created AFTER load,
# which is what proves live access rather than a snapshot -- and
# check 8 holds stage 1's lifetime semantics for query results:
# a pre-navigation query handle goes inert, fresh queries answer
# the second child's tree, and after the frame's removal the
# queries answer empty. The page scored 5/8 before this patch
# (checks 7 and 8 missing their surface, and check 4's new
# query-handle inertness leg with them). In Firefox/Camoufox
# checks 1-3, 6 and 7 pass -- the query surface behaves as a
# browser's does; 4, 5 and 8 are doc 19's handle semantics,
# where a browser's captured Document stays with its old page.
# JS-frame-xdoc.html stays XDOC 4/4 and JS-xorigin-frame.html
# stays XFRAME 9/9; the cross-origin null is untouched.
# Simulator only; the vita cross-build was not run (no vitaSDK
# on this VM). Device expectation is in Doc 10.
